WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:05.120
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:10.760
Today we are going to build the best virtual
toaster that you have ever seen.

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:15.860
And it’s going to be so easy that it hardly
seems possible.

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:22.280
All this technique asks for is our input geometry
to be a collection of parametric shapes.

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:27.850
I’ll tell you in a moment what that is,
but for now, let’s see that toaster.

00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:33.640
Hmm, this looks fine, but what if we feel
that it is not quite tall enough?

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:36.650
Well, with this technique, look!

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:43.460
It can change the height of it, which is fantastic,
but something else also happens!

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:53.319
Look, it also understands the body’s relation
to other objects that are connected to it.

00:00:53.319 --> 00:01:01.440
We can also change the location of the handle,
the slits can be adjusted symmetrically, and

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:07.380
when we move the toasts, it understands that
it moves together with the handles.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:14.840
This is super useful, for instance, have a
look at this train example, where if we change

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:20.910
the wheels, it also understands that not only
the wheels, but the wheel wells also have

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:24.990
to change as well.

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:29.360
This concept also works really well on this
curtain.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:34.860
And all this means that we can not only dream
up and execute these changes ourselves without

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:41.780
having to ask a trained artist, but we can
also do it super quickly and efficiently.

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:43.420
Loving it.

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:49.170
Just to demonstrate how profound and non-trivial
this understanding of interrelations is, here

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:52.140
is an example of a complex object.

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:58.860
Without this technique, if we grab one thing,
exactly that one thing moves, which is represented

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:01.670
by one of these sliders changing here.

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:07.240
However, if we would grab this contraption
in the real world, not only one thing would

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:11.280
move, nearly every part would move at the
same time.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:15.280
So, does this new method know that?

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:19.830
Oh wow, it does, look at that!

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:26.329
And, at the same time, not one, but many sliders
are dancing around beautifully.

00:02:26.329 --> 00:02:32.719
Now, I mentioned that the requirement was
that the input object has to be a parametric

00:02:32.719 --> 00:02:38.331
shape, this is something that can be generated
from intuitive parameters, for instance, we

00:02:38.331 --> 00:02:43.549
can generate a circle if we say what the radius
of the circle should be.

00:02:43.549 --> 00:02:48.780
The radius would be the parameter here, and
the resulting circle is hence a parametric

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:49.870
shape.

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:55.480
In many domains, this is standard procedure,
for instance, many computer aided design systems

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:57.889
work with parametric objects.

00:02:57.889 --> 00:03:01.450
But we are not done yet, not even close!

00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:06.709
It also understands how the brush sizes that
we use relates to our thinking.

00:03:06.709 --> 00:03:08.120
Don’t believe it?

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:09.889
Let’s have a look together!

00:03:09.889 --> 00:03:16.749
Right after we click, it detects that we have
a small brush size, and therefore infers that

00:03:16.749 --> 00:03:22.060
we probably wish to do something with the
handle, and, there we go!

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:24.080
That is really cool.

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:31.180
And now, let’s increase the brush size and
click nearby, and, bam!

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:32.180
There we go!

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:36.909
Now it knows that we wish to interact with
the drawer.

00:03:36.909 --> 00:03:38.090
Same with the doors.

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:44.299
And, hold on to your papers, because to demonstrate
the utility of their technique, the authors

00:03:44.299 --> 00:03:47.599
also made a scene just for us.

00:03:47.599 --> 00:03:48.859
Look!

00:03:48.859 --> 00:03:54.159
Nice - this is no less than a Two Minute Papers
branded chronometer!

00:03:54.159 --> 00:04:00.569
And here, we can change the proportions, the
dial, the hands, whatever we wish, and it

00:04:00.569 --> 00:04:06.140
is so easy and showcases the utility of the
new method so well.

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:14.840
Now, I know what you’re thinking, let’s
see it ticking…and…will it be two minutes?

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:16.389
Well, close enough.

00:04:16.389 --> 00:04:22.479
Certainly, much closer to two minutes than
the length of these videos, that is for sure.

00:04:22.479 --> 00:04:27.031
Thank you so much for the authors for taking
time off their busy day just to make this.

00:04:27.031 --> 00:04:35.120
So, this truly is a wonderful tool because
even a novice artist without 3D modeling expertise

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:39.690
can apply meaningful changes to complex pieces
of geometry.

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:42.450
No trained artist is required!

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:43.610
What a time to be alive!

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:49.889
Now, this didn’t quite fit anywhere in this
video, but I really wanted to show you this

00:04:49.889 --> 00:04:55.250
heartwarming message from Mark Chen, research
scientist at OpenAI.

00:04:55.250 --> 00:05:00.200
This really showcases one of the best parts
of my job, and that is when the authors of

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:04.889
the paper come in and enjoy the results with
you Fellow Scholars.

00:05:04.889 --> 00:05:06.470
Loving it.

00:05:06.470 --> 00:05:08.370
Thank you so much again!

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:13.479
Also make sure to check out Yannic’s channel
for cool in-depth videos on machine learning

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:14.479
works.

